<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  kidnix: changing the shape of the sandbox.

  /etc/kidnix/parent.toml and /etc/kidnix/session.toml decide who the children
  are, how long a sitting is, what a child may open and how the machine sounds.
  They are root-owned because the shell runs as the child, and a child-writable
  session length is not a session length. The parent panel runs as the PARENT,
  who is in wheel and is not root, so it cannot write them either; and this
  is the action behind the helper that can (/usr/bin/kidnix-config).

  ONE CALLER, ONE ROUTE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.

  * A grown-up on their own desktop meets the defaults below:
    allow_active=auth_admin_keep, i.e. a wheel password. That is the stock
    Fedora admin experience, the same shape as org.kidnix.parent-tools, and not
    a new secret to remember. auth_admin_KEEP because setting a machine up is
    six tabs and one Apply, and being asked again five minutes later teaches a
    parent to stop reading the dialogue.

  * THE CHILD'S SESSION IS NOT A CALLER. Unlike org.kidnix.set-pin, this action
    gets NO carve-out in /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/40-kidnix-kid.rules: the
    "org.kidnix." prefix in that file's deny list already covers it, and it
    must stay covered. Adding this id to that file's one-item allow-list would
    hand a five-year-old the ability to lengthen their own session, empty their
    own bedtime and tick every activity back on. Nothing about the first-PIN
    argument that justifies set-pin applies here: a machine with no settings
    yet has working defaults, and there is no screen a child is trapped on.

  * allow_inactive=no keeps it off ssh-without-a-session and off the greeter.

  THE AUTHORISATION IS NOT THE AUTHORITY. Saying yes here grants the right to
  *ask*; /usr/bin/kidnix-config decides. It re-checks that the caller is root
  or in wheel from PKEXEC_UID; it validates the payload against the panel's own
  rules; it parses the files it is about to install with the SHELL's own reader
  and refuses anything the child's screen could not read back; it writes
  atomically; and it will not set a PIN; the hash and salt are copied off the
  file that already exists and put back unchanged, because changing a PIN is
  org.kidnix.set-pin's job and that is the path that demands the current one
  and rate-limits guesses.

  pkexec matches an action to a program by the exec.path annotation below.

  NOTE FOR EDITORS: this is an XML comment, so it may not contain a double
  hyphen anywhere. tests/image/test_parent_panel.sh parses this file to make
  sure a well-meant edit cannot unregister the action or loosen its defaults.
-->
<policyconfig>
  <vendor>kidnix</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/mattcree/kidnix</vendor_url>

  <action id="org.kidnix.parent-config">
    <description>Change this machine's settings for a child</description>
    <description xml:lang="en_GB">Change this machine's settings for a child</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to change the children, time limits and activities on this machine</message>
    <message xml:lang="en_GB">Authentication is required to change the children, time limits and activities on this machine</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/bin/kidnix-config</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.allow_gui">true</annotate>
  </action>
</policyconfig>
